# GitHub Version Tag Release

Fetch [the live-reference entry guide](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/davidtaylor6130/My-AI-Skills/main/skills/Github/references/using-references.md), then follow its shared context
and action policy. A request to prepare a release does not approve a tag push or publication.

## Version and target

Read [David's versioning rules](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/davidtaylor6130/My-AI-Skills/main/skills/Github/references/versioning.md). Assess the actual proposed changes and agree
the exact `X.Y.Z` candidate, local alpha/beta build identifiers and intended public identifier.
Follow that reference's inventory checks and exceptional prerelease confirmations; do not substitute
generic semantic-version assumptions or automatically publish a development suffix.

If version/README/package edits are needed, prepare and verify them locally. Use [the commit
workflow](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/davidtaylor6130/My-AI-Skills/main/skills/Github/references/commits.md) and [Create PR](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/davidtaylor6130/My-AI-Skills/main/skills/Github/github-create-pr/SKILL.md) when integration needs a PR.
Wait for the approved integrated revision. Confirm the final release branch/revision, previous tag
and actual change range after those edits; neither uncommitted work nor an unmerged bump is included
in a release candidate by assumption. Verify the target is available on the intended remote.

## Write the release notes

Follow [the release-notes format](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/davidtaylor6130/My-AI-Skills/main/skills/Github/references/release-format.md) for the short project explanation, changes,
known issues, environment, actual Proof/Review and model/app/harness attribution. Read relevant open
issues and linked change evidence; state any unavailable or incomplete inventory. Verify the notes
against the selected release revision and [the shared writing guide](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/davidtaylor6130/My-AI-Skills/main/skills/Github/references/writing-guide.md).

Keep the project's existing changelog format when adding an entry. A new changelog/README edit
changes the candidate; return through commit/integration and revision validation before tagging.
Select actual release assets and check their filenames, embedded/package versions and validation;
a plain tag does not make an alpha/beta binary a stable release.

## Review and publication

Use [the shared publishing workflow](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/davidtaylor6130/My-AI-Skills/main/skills/Github/references/publishing.md) for exact notes files, remote approval,
verification and retry recovery. Show the exact tag, target revision, title, notes, assets and
planned draft/published/prerelease state for David's review. Preserve the repository's agreed tag
scheme and use an annotated tag when creating a new one.

Check existing local/remote tags and releases before creating anything. Do not overwrite, move or
recreate them to bypass a conflict. Create any approved annotated tag at the exact approved revision,
then perform only the approved tag push. Before release creation, verify that the remote tag exists
and resolves to that same commit; for an annotated tag, compare its peeled commit rather than its
tag-object ID. Stop if the push fails or the remote tag is missing or points elsewhere.

Use the shared publishing workflow's existing-tag and draft-state safeguards for release creation
and later publication. Those are distinct effects governed by the action policy; perform only the
approved stages. Recheck the remote target, release fields and downloadable assets, then return the
verified URL and remaining gaps. Do not merge or push other branches as an implicit step.
